<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Orbit/Resources/Info.plist

	Deliberately minimal: `GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE = YES` stays on for the
	`Orbit` target, so every simple key (CFBundleDisplayName, version
	strings, icon, …) keeps coming from this target's `INFOPLIST_KEY_*`
	build settings and asset catalog, exactly as before. This file exists
	only for the handful of dictionary/array-shaped keys `INFOPLIST_KEY_*`
	has no way to express:

	- `UTExportedTypeDeclarations` — the two custom drag-and-drop
	  `UTType`s Orbit creates with `UTType(exportedAs:)`
	  (`Orbit/UI/Sidebar/SidebarDragDrop.swift`,
	  `Orbit/UI/Spaces/SpaceSwitcherPagerView.swift`) are a promise that
	  the app declares them here; without a matching entry macOS warns at
	  runtime ("Type ... was expected to be declared") and only partially
	  registers the type, which can make drag-and-drop behave
	  inconsistently.
	- `CFBundleURLTypes` / `CFBundleDocumentTypes` — what makes macOS
	  offer Orbit in System Settings' "Default web browser" list at all
	  (`Orbit/Core/DefaultBrowser.swift`'s own header comment already
	  documented this dependency before it was wired up).
	-->
	<!--
	`NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription` is what macOS shows in the
	permission prompt Live Calendars triggers
	(`Orbit/Features/LiveCalendars/LiveCalendarStore.swift`). It is a plain
	string and could in principle be an `INFOPLIST_KEY_*` build setting, but
	it lives here so the whole Live Calendars surface can be added without
	editing `project.pbxproj`. Without this key, `requestFullAccessToEvents`
	does not merely fail — TCC terminates the process.
	-->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Orbit reads your calendar to show the next meeting and a Join button in the sidebar. Nothing is sent anywhere — it is read on this Mac and shown to you.</string>
	<!--
	THE REST OF THE USAGE DESCRIPTIONS BELOW ARE NOT OPTIONAL POLISH.

	Orbit is not sandboxed (`ENABLE_APP_SANDBOX = NO`) and runs under the
	hardened runtime, so every capability below is gated by TCC. TCC's
	contract is the one `NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription` above already
	documents: a process that touches a protected resource with **no usage
	description for it** is not denied, it is **terminated**. Missing keys
	here are therefore not "a permission Orbit forgot to ask for" — they are
	a crash the first time an ordinary user does an ordinary thing.

	Each key below is here because Orbit has the code that reaches the
	protected resource. Nothing is declared speculatively; a key for a
	capability Orbit does not have would be its own defect (it makes
	System Settings offer Orbit a switch that controls nothing).

	Arc declared the same set and for the same stated reason. Read directly
	off `/Applications/Arc.app/Contents/Info.plist`:

	    NSCameraUsageDescription        "Websites may use your camera for
	                                     features on their site."
	    NSMicrophoneUsageDescription    "Websites may use your microphone …"
	    NSLocationUsageDescription      "Websites may use your location …"
	    NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription   (same string)
	    NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription
	                                    "Access to \"Downloads\" is required
	                                     for downloading files from the web
	                                     and to make files accessible from
	                                     Arc."

	One key Arc declared is deliberately NOT copied here, because Orbit has
	no code that reaches it and an inert privacy switch is exactly the kind
	of control this project has shipped and regretted before:

	  - `NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription` — Arc read the Desktop for its own
	    file surfaces. Nothing in Orbit reads or writes `~/Desktop`
	    (`grep -rn "desktopDirectory"` across the app returns nothing).

	`NSLocationAlwaysUsageDescription` and
	`NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription`, which Arc also declared,
	are likewise omitted: they describe background location, which is an iOS
	authorisation level. macOS resolves a browser's geolocation request to
	when-in-use, and the two keys below are the ones CoreLocation actually
	reads on this platform.
	-->
	<!--
	A page calling `getUserMedia({ video: true })`, mapped to
	`PermissionKind.camera`. Orbit always asks the user first
	(`AppEnvironment+WebContentsDelegate.swift`), so this string describes
	the only thing that can happen: a site asked, and the user said yes.
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>A website you open can ask to use your camera — for a video call, say. Orbit always asks you before letting one, and remembers your answer for that site. No page gets your camera on its own.</string>
	<!--
	Same path as camera, for `getUserMedia({ audio: true })`, landing on
	`PermissionKind.microphone`.
	-->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>A website you open can ask to use your microphone — for a video call, say. Orbit always asks you before letting one, and remembers your answer for that site. No page gets your microphone on its own.</string>
	<!--
	`PermissionKind.geolocation`, surfaced in the Site Control popover as
	the `Location` row. The macOS position provider is CoreLocation, so
	granting a site this permission is what reaches TCC.
	`NSLocationUsageDescription` is the older key macOS still consults
	alongside the when-in-use one; both carry the same sentence so the
	panel reads identically whichever the system picks.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>A website you open can ask where you are — a map or a store locator, say. Orbit always asks you before telling one, and remembers your answer for that site.</string>
	<!--
	Web Bluetooth: a page calling `navigator.bluetooth.requestDevice()`.
	Not reached through Orbit's own permission bridge — the browser engine
	can expose this capability on its own, so the key stays declared even
	though no Orbit Swift code asks for it. Without it, the first page to
	call that API terminates Orbit, the same crash class as the keys above.
	-->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>A website you open can ask to talk to a nearby Bluetooth device — a fitness tracker or a games controller, say. Orbit always asks you before letting one, and remembers your answer for that site.</string>
	<key>NSLocationUsageDescription</key>
	<string>A website you open can ask where you are — a map or a store locator, say. Orbit always asks you before telling one, and remembers your answer for that site.</string>
	<!--
	`AppEnvironment+WebContentsDelegate.swift`'s `willBeginDownload` resolves
	`FileManager.SearchPathDirectory.downloadsDirectory` and writes the file
	there. `~/Downloads` is TCC-protected for an unsandboxed app, so without
	this key the FIRST DOWNLOAD A USER EVER STARTS terminates Orbit. There is
	no user action that avoids it and no setting that moves the destination.
	-->
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Orbit saves the files you download from the web into your Downloads folder, the same place every other browser puts them.</string>
	<!--
	SPARKLE, THE IN-APP UPDATER.

	The four keys below are plain strings, booleans and numbers, so on the
	rule stated at the top of this file they could each have been an
	`INFOPLIST_KEY_*` build setting instead. They are here on purpose. Two of
	them are not configuration, they are a trust decision: `SUFeedURL` names
	the one server Orbit will accept new code from, and `SUPublicEDKey` names
	the one key that code must be signed with. A decision of that shape needs
	the paragraphs below written beside it, and a build setting has nowhere
	to put a paragraph. Sparkle reads all four through `SUHost`, which reads
	the built bundle's Info.plist, so it makes no difference to Sparkle which
	route they take.

	Everything Sparkle can be told through this file, and is not told here,
	is deliberately left at its own default:

	  `SUAutomaticallyUpdate` (default NO) stays unset, so Orbit checks for
	  updates on its own but never installs one without the user agreeing.
	  Silently replacing a browser under a user who is in the middle of
	  something is not a thing this app does.

	  `SUShowReleaseNotes` (default YES) stays unset: the appcast carries
	  release notes and the user should see what is about to change.

	  `SUVerifyUpdateBeforeExtraction`, `SURequireSignedFeed`,
	  `SUAllowsAutomaticUpdates`, `SUDefaultsDomain` and
	  `SUEnableSystemProfiling` (all default NO or unset) stay unset. The
	  last one matters most: system profiling appends a description of this
	  Mac to every update check, and an updater that reports the machine it
	  runs on is not something a browser should do without asking.

	  `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` and `SUEnableDownloaderService`
	  (both default NO) stay unset, and their two XPC services are stripped
	  out of the embedded framework entirely. Sparkle needs them only to
	  reach past an App Sandbox, and Orbit has no App Sandbox
	  (ENABLE_APP_SANDBOX = NO). See `Scripts/release_manager.py`,
	  SPARKLE_XPC_NOTE, for the whole argument and for what happens if a
	  future change turns either key on.

	There is no Info.plist key for update channels, so nothing here opts
	Orbit into pre-releases. That is done in code, by returning a channel
	name from `SPUUpdaterDelegate.allowedChannels(for:)` and matching it
	against a `<sparkle:channel>` element in the appcast.
	-->
	<!--
	Where Orbit looks for new versions.

	A single cumulative appcast, served from GitHub Pages off this
	repository's `gh-pages` branch, carrying both the stable and the beta
	items. Sparkle chooses between them on the client, by matching the
	`<sparkle:channel>` element on an item against the set returned from
	`SPUUpdaterDelegate.allowedChannels(for:)`. A build that returns nothing
	from that method sees only the unchannelled, stable items, which is what
	makes one feed safe to serve to everybody.

	This was NOT the obvious choice, and the obvious choice is wrong. The
	tempting URL is a GitHub release asset reached through
	`/releases/latest/download/appcast.xml`, which needs nothing hosted and
	rewrites itself at every release. It cannot carry a beta. GitHub defines
	the "latest" release as the most recent one that is neither a draft nor
	a pre-release, so an appcast attached to a pre-release is unreachable
	through that path by construction: the beta channel would exist in the
	code, be advertised in the settings, and never once deliver an update.
	The redirect is not merely awkward for pre-releases, it excludes them.

	The cost of the cumulative feed is that it has somewhere to be served
	from. GitHub Pages has to be switched on for this repository, once, by
	hand, and pointed at `gh-pages`; until that is done this URL returns 404
	and no copy of Orbit can update. That is a maintainer step, not a build
	step, and refs/RELEASING.md is where it is written down.

	HTTPS is not optional. Sparkle logs "The feed URL may need to change to
	use HTTPS" for anything else, and an update feed fetched over plain HTTP
	is an invitation to serve a user a different browser than the one they
	installed.
	-->
	<key>SUFeedURL</key>
	<string>https://seggys116.github.io/Orbit/appcast.xml</string>
	<!--
	THE ED25519 PUBLIC KEY EVERY UPDATE MUST BE SIGNED WITH.

	Generated 2026-08-12, `generate_keys` account `orbit`. Private half: the
	maintainer's login keychain, and the SPARKLE_PRIVATE_KEY secret CI signs
	with. Lose both and no future release can ever update an existing install.

	Never remove it. Sparkle 2.9.5 with no key does not refuse to update: it
	logs a deprecation, ignores the appcast's EdDSA signature and accepts the
	download on Apple code signing alone. Absent fails open; a key that will
	not decode fails closed. Scripts/release doctor and verify reject both.
	-->
	<key>SUPublicEDKey</key>
	<string>rssJhOsAtL/2xwfnxWRLlZlWztQnBVvGRAE5jDbZDzQ=</string>
	<!--
	Check for updates on a schedule, without asking first.

	Left unset, Sparkle asks the user on second launch whether it may check
	automatically. Orbit answers that question for them, which is a real
	decision and not a default worth inheriting: Orbit is a browser, the
	thing it is a wrapper around is Chromium, and Chromium's security fixes
	are the reason to run a current one. An Orbit that is a version behind
	is a Chromium that is a version behind, on the process that parses
	whatever the web hands it. A browser that has to be asked for permission
	to notice that is the wrong shape.

	This sets the default, not a lock. Sparkle resolves the same key out of
	the app's user defaults before falling back to this file, so a Settings
	toggle writing `SPUUpdater.automaticallyChecksForUpdates` persists and
	wins from then on.

	Checking is all this enables. `SUAutomaticallyUpdate` is unset above, so
	Orbit still asks before it downloads or installs anything.
	-->
	<key>SUEnableAutomaticChecks</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	Six hours between scheduled checks, rather than Sparkle's default of one
	day.

	Sparkle only checks while the app is running, and a browser is the
	application most likely to be left open for days at a time. On the
	default interval, a Chromium respin published to fix an actively
	exploited bug could sit unnoticed for the better part of a day by a user
	who never quits Orbit. Six hours bounds that. The cost is four
	conditional GETs a day against a static file on GitHub's CDN, which is
	not a cost.

	Sparkle clamps anything below one hour, so this is well inside what it
	will honour, and it is the interval between *scheduled* checks only: the
	menu item still checks on demand.
	-->
	<key>SUScheduledCheckInterval</key>
	<integer>21600</integer>
	<key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
